> This seems ridiculous to me. 

In English, you can write things like "Verbing weirds
language."  Thereby taking two nouns, making verbs
out of them (which is against the rules of proper 
grammar) and then taking one of those new verbs and
making it back into a gerund noun and slamming them
both into a three-word sentence, which, makes a
delightfully funny, and ironic, point, (since
the crimes you have just committed is called 'verbing'.)

That's a powerful language. That's flexibility.

Another example is Lewis Carrol's poem Jabberwocky,
which is full of nonsense words and rare words, but
most students can still scan it, even though they
don't know what the words mean: "Twas brillig and
the slithy toves did gyre and...."


Nobody knows what "brillig" means, no matter how
many Jabberwocks they kill.

Now some folks are posting that English will recede, but
the thing that makes a language die out is rigidity,
such as what the French Canadians are doing.  I would
claim that the very laws they pass trying to preserve
the French language in Quebec is what's going to kill
it in the long run.  And to a much lesser extent, the
French are doing the same thing by resisting the influx
of English words (I cite the recent news article where
they are trying to replace "e-mail" with some other
word.)

But English is no where near doing such things.  There
are pidgins of English cropping up everywhere, and second
generations speaking creols, and while these are inferior
languages, (stilted and limited in vocabulary) they will 
grow up to be part of the evolution of English, giving
and taking, and breathing ever more life into it.  

English is huge and has lots of contributors and is very
vital.  If it's going to die out, it won't be for a 
very long time.



